# Dropbox App Console (https://www.dropbox.com/developers/apps) app's client
# id ("App key") - set up as a "Scoped access" app with "App folder" access,
# scopes `files.content.write`/`files.content.read`. This is a public OAuth
# PKCE client id, not a secret - it's safe to ship in built JS and doesn't
# need to stay out of version control on its own, but it's still kept in a
# real, gitignored `.env` (copy this file to `.env` and fill it in) rather
# than committed directly, so per-environment (dev/prod) redirect URIs stay
# straightforward to manage.
VITE_DROPBOX_CLIENT_ID=your-dropbox-app-key-here

# Azure AD (Microsoft Entra) app registration's "Application (client) ID"
# (https://portal.azure.com) - the platform type MUST be "Single-page
# application", not "Web" (the "Web" platform type blocks browser-origin PKCE
# token exchange since it expects a confidential client with a secret).
# Supports "Accounts in any organizational directory and personal Microsoft
# accounts", scope `Files.ReadWrite.AppFolder`. Same public-PKCE-client
# non-secret status as the Dropbox key above.
VITE_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=

# Google Cloud Console (https://console.cloud.google.com) OAuth 2.0 Client ID
# of type "Web application", with the Drive API enabled and the OAuth
# consent screen scoped to `drive.appdata`. Same public-PKCE-client
# non-secret status as the keys above. Note: while the consent screen's
# publishing status is "Testing" (the default until Google's app-verification
# process is completed), issued refresh tokens expire after 7 days and only
# up to 100 test users can connect - see cloud-sync-page.ts's Google Drive
# "may need reconnecting periodically" note, which is why this ships as beta.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
